# Line endings, declared rather than left to whatever each machine has
# configured.
#
# This is not tidiness. GitHub's windows-latest runners check out with
# core.autocrlf=true, so a repository that says nothing gets CRLF in the working
# tree — and `gofmt -l` reports every CRLF file as needing formatting, because
# gofmt only ever writes LF. The first CI run on this repository failed for that
# reason and only that reason: all 66 Go files flagged, on a tree that is
# silent under gofmt on the machine it was written on.
#
# Declaring eol=lf makes the checkout the same everywhere and takes the local
# core.autocrlf setting out of the question. It also settles the same hazard for
# views.sql, which is read and searched by tests: a check for ";\n" finds
# nothing in a file checked out as ";\r\n", which is a defect that has already
# been hit once here.

# Everything git recognises as text is normalised to LF in the repository.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The formats where the ending is not cosmetic.
*.go   text eol=lf
*.sql  text eol=lf
*.html text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.md   text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf
*.txt  text eol=lf

# Binary, so git never rewrites a byte of them. The .syso is a COFF object the
# linker consumes and the icon is its payload; a line-ending conversion inside
# either would corrupt the executable's resources in a way that builds cleanly
# and only shows up as a missing icon.
*.png  binary
*.ico  binary
*.syso binary
*.exe  binary
*.duckdb binary
*.pf   binary
*.evtx binary
